## Configuration

Every setting is an environment variable, read once at startup. There is no
configuration file and no `.env` loading in the application itself.
`.env.example` lists every variable with a safe placeholder and says what
each one does.

Production fails closed: `APP_ENV=production` refuses to start with `DEBUG`
enabled, with a non-JSON log format, or bound to every interface without
`ALLOW_PUBLIC_BIND=true`, or without `DATABASE_URL`. Every
violation is reported at once, so a failed startup names everything to fix.

The PostgreSQL pool defaults to at most 10 connections, creates no idle
connections eagerly, and recycles connections after 30 minutes. The complete
set of bounds is in `.env.example`. Size `DB_MAX_CONNS` against
PostgreSQL's limit across every application replica rather than per process
in isolation. A password may be supplied separately through
`DATABASE_PASSWORD` or `DATABASE_PASSWORD_FILE`; this avoids
putting URL-reserved secret characters into `DATABASE_URL`.

Any secret may be supplied as `<NAME>_FILE` naming a file whose contents are
the value, for an orchestrator's mounted-file secret store. Setting both
`<NAME>` and `<NAME>_FILE` is always an error.

## Database migrations

Migration SQL remains readable under `db/migrations/` and is embedded in the
binary through `db.Migrations()`. The generated migrator is instance-scoped,
uses a PostgreSQL advisory lock, and never runs merely because the server
starts. `00001_baseline.sql` is intentionally schema-neutral: it establishes
Goose version history without guessing at application tables.

The database integration harness uses `TEST_DATABASE_URL` as an administrative
connection to a disposable PostgreSQL server. It creates a unique database per
test, caps it at eight connections, and force-drops it during cleanup. Local
absence skips; `CI=true` or `CI=1` makes absence fail. The configured role needs
`CREATEDB`; never point it at a production cluster. The migration test applies
and rolls back the complete embedded migration set inside its isolated database.
`make migration-test` additionally proves a clean install and forward migration
from every immutable release-schema SQL fixture under
`internal/platform/database/testdata/migration-upgrades/`.

Inspect and migrate the configured database explicitly:

```sh
nise db status
nise db migrate
```

The same application-owned surface is available without Nise as
`go run ./cmd/{{.AppName}} db status` and `... db migrate`. Status is
read-only and does not create the Goose history table on a fresh database.
Startup allows an empty or behind schema with a warning, but fails closed for
ahead, malformed, partial, or missing history. Migration is never a server
startup side effect.

Feature-local `sqlc.yaml` files enable the canonical
`nise-no-unbounded-delete`, `nise-no-unbounded-update`, and
`nise-no-truncate` CEL rules. Run `make sqlc-vet` to evaluate them with pinned
sqlc and to reject any generated Go output outside that feature's `store/`.
The target is an explicit no-op before the first query-owning feature exists
and is included in `make all`.

## Database transactions

Business use cases own transaction boundaries through
`internal/platform/database.Transactor`. Its callback receives a concrete
`pgx.Tx`, which is passed directly to the feature-local sqlc query constructor;
repositories never begin or commit. Successful work commits, while errors and
cancellation roll back through a detached but bounded cleanup context. Panic
rolls back and re-panics unchanged. Propagate the callback context: a nested
`Within` call made with it fails instead of creating a hidden savepoint or
independent inner commit.

Direct pgx is reserved for reviewed PostgreSQL operations sqlc cannot model,
such as `CopyFrom`. Keep the adapter beside its owning feature, accept only the
tracer-covered methods it needs, use static SQL and identifiers with bound
values, and construct it from the use case callback's `pgx.Tx`. The
compile-checked `internal/platform/database/directpgx_test.go` demonstrates the
shape and proves commit/rollback behavior against disposable PostgreSQL; it is
an example, not a shared raw repository.

## Database query instrumentation

The server pool installs an application-owned pgx tracer. It counts and times
ordinary queries, copies, and batches under closed low-cardinality labels and
exports `db_queries_total` plus `db_query_duration_seconds` through the
operator metrics endpoint. `DB_SLOW_QUERY_THRESHOLD` defaults to `250ms` and
must be positive. Slow warnings contain only the bounded statement class,
outcome, duration, threshold, and available validated correlation IDs—never
SQL, arguments, copied rows, results, or database errors.

Reference-flow tests can derive a context with `database.WithQueryBudget`,
assert its exact `Count`, and call `Verify` for the maximum. A query count is a
pgx round trip: a query/exec, a copy, or an entire batch each counts once.
Batch users separately bound queued statements because one round trip can
still ask PostgreSQL to do substantial work.

## Process modes

The same binary runs every process this application has.

| Mode | HTTP server | Worker | Port bound |
|---|---|---|---|
| `all` (default) | yes | yes | yes |
| `web` | yes | no | yes |
| `worker` | no | yes | **no** |

```sh
go run ./cmd/{{.AppName}} -mode web
```

`db status` and `db migrate` are commands, not process modes: they run one
bounded database operation and exit without starting HTTP or worker loops.

Under `all`, if either component stops, the other is shut down before the
process exits.

## API routing

`/api/v1` is an exact JSON API boundary. Register routes relative to that
prefix through `httpapi.Deps.RegisterAPI`; `/widgets/{id}` becomes
`/api/v1/widgets/{id}`. A nil callback is a valid empty API, and unknown API
paths receive an API-policy `404` rather than falling through to the embedded
SPA.

The API and document surfaces are sibling chains with independently built
security policies. Both run security, request identity/logging, recovery, and
metrics before the matching application middleware slot and route dispatch.
Add cross-cutting application middleware only through
`httpapi.MiddlewareSlots`; entries run in slice order inside the fixed core.
`internal/platform/httpapi/router_test.go` is application-owned and locks this
boundary so deliberate changes can update code and contract together.

`api/openapi.yaml` is authoritative and its paths are relative to `/api/v1`.
After editing it, run `make api-generate`; `make api-check` performs a
non-mutating byte comparison and is part of `make all`. Application handlers
implement the generated `openapigen.StrictServerInterface` through
`internal/platform/httpapi/api.go`. The initial `GET /api/v1/` returns the
direct `{ "version": "v1" }` API-root resource, not health or dependency
state. Single resources are returned directly. Collections define concrete,
closed `{ "items": [...], "page": {...} }` schemas with `[]` for no items;
do not introduce a generic `data`, `result`, or runtime envelope.

Operational lists page by cursor: `limit`, `after`, and `before` in, a
`CursorPage` out, with `next_cursor` and `prev_cursor` absent rather than null
when there is no page that way. A cursor is an opaque HMAC-authenticated token
that expires after `CURSOR_TTL`, is bound to the exact query it was issued for,
and may only be used in the direction it was issued for. Set
`CURSOR_SIGNING_KEY` to `<id>:<base64 secret>`; production refuses to start
without it, and `CURSOR_RETIRED_KEYS` rotates it without breaking outstanding
cursors. `runtime/pagination` owns the token; `api.go` owns the per-collection
binding, page issuing, and `400` mapping.

The session cookie is `__Host-session`, `Secure`, `HttpOnly`, `SameSite=Lax`,
`Path=/`, with no `Domain`, in **every** environment. `http://localhost` and
`http://127.0.0.1` are secure contexts in every current browser, so development
runs the production cookie path rather than a weaker branch that only users
would find bugs in. `SESSION_COOKIE_INSECURE=true` is the single escape hatch,
for a development origin that is not localhost; it drops `Secure` and the
`__Host-` prefix together because a browser discards a `__Host-` cookie that is
not `Secure`, it warns at startup, and production refuses to start with it set.

`internal/platform/httpapi/transport_test.go` is the end-to-end contract over
the real handler: routing, negotiation, strict input, pagination, idempotency,
and response bounds, plus a fuzz target that holds every API response to a
status in range, the security headers, valid request identity, the Problem
media type on failure, and no Go implementation detail in the body. A request
whose method is outside chi's own table is dispatched into the matching
surface's core rather than answered before the chain runs, so an unusual verb
cannot produce the one response with no security headers.

Registration is closed. Create the first administrator on an empty database
with `<app> admin bootstrap --email you@example.com`; it prints an invitation
token once and refuses once any account exists. Everyone else is invited by
somebody holding `users.manage`. Rerunning the bootstrap while the directory is
still empty withdraws the earlier link, so a lost token is recoverable without
leaving a second unauthenticated way in.

A command worth not repeating takes an `Idempotency-Key` header.
`internal/platform/idempotency` writes its record in the same transaction as
the command, so a rolled-back command leaves no claim and a visible record is
always a completed one. A retry with the same key replays the recorded response
byte-for-byte; a second attempt arriving while the first is still running is
refused with `409` through a transaction-scoped advisory lock rather than held
open; a key replayed with a different body is `422`. Retention defaults to 24
hours and expired records are removed by a bounded sweep.

Reports use a separate `page`/`size` contract returning `ReportPage` with
`total` and `total_pages`. Offsets are computed in `int64`, page and size are
refused rather than clamped, a page past the report's offset ceiling is
`report_too_deep`, and a request mixing report and cursor parameters is
refused. Use cursors for anything a user scrolls; use a report only when the
page number is part of the answer.

Declared JSON request bodies are limited to 1 MiB and require the exact
declared media type (parameters are allowed), one value, no unknown fields,
and no repeated object keys, including case variants at any nesting level.
JSON container nesting is limited to 100 levels.
Failures map to RFC 9457 Problem Details for `400`, `413`, or `415` without
exposing the decoder cause. The same controlled shape handles API `404`,
`405`, generated `500`, and recovered panic responses. It contains stable
public fields plus only `code`, `request_id`, and `correlation_id`; add new
extensions through `api/openapi.yaml`, never an arbitrary map. Definitions
and the bounded writer live in `internal/platform/httpapi/problem`. API
recovery stages at most 1 MiB so a write-then-panic becomes one clean Problem;
explicitly flushed/hijacked streams pass through and are aborted, never
followed by appended JSON, if they later panic.
Generated buffered JSON responses, Problems, and `httpjson.Write` are limited
to 1 MiB before headers are committed; streaming and non-JSON responses are
not buffered by this policy.

The same document generates `frontend/src/lib/api/schema.d.ts`; use
`make api-types-generate` and `make api-types-check`. The application-owned
`client.ts` restricts calls to generated paths and methods, sends explicit
same-origin credentials, forwards cancellation, and maps failures without
putting bodies or causes into public error messages. `make all` type-checks
and runs this client contract after the frontend dependencies are installed.

## Health probes

| Path | Wire it to |
|---|---|
| `/healthz/startup` | Your orchestrator's startup probe |
| `/healthz/live` | Your orchestrator's liveness probe — its failure restarts the process |
| `/healthz/ready` | Your load balancer's health check — its failure stops traffic without restarting |

Each answers with a status code and one of two fixed bodies, and never names
a dependency. Shutdown fails readiness first, waits a drain period so the
load balancer's next poll observes it, and only then stops accepting
connections.

## Operator endpoints

`/internal/metrics` (Prometheus text format) and
`/internal/healthz/ready/report` (the per-check readiness detail) are mounted
only when `OPERATOR_TOKEN` is set, and always behind it. Call them with
`Authorization: Bearer $OPERATOR_TOKEN`. Without the header they answer 404,
so an unauthorized caller cannot learn that they exist.

Prefer binding them to a private network path in your deployment. Metrics
reveal request volume, timing, and route topology that the health probes
deliberately withhold.

## Layout

```text
api/                      authoritative OpenAPI contract and generation config
cmd/{{.AppName}}/         the binary: flag parsing and mode selection
db/                       readable SQL migrations and their generated embed package
deploy/                   Dockerfile and compose file
frontend/                 the SvelteKit project — see frontend/README.md
internal/app/             constructor wiring and process modes
internal/features/        one directory per vertical slice
internal/platform/        technical infrastructure this application owns
```

`internal/` has exactly three children. A domain rule belongs in a feature,
never in `platform/`. There is no `internal/util/`, `internal/common/`,
`internal/shared/`, or `internal/pkg/`.

## Ownership

Every file Nise wrote carries one of two headers:

```text
Code generated by nise; DO NOT EDIT.
Generated once by nise; owned by this application. Nise will not overwrite it.
```

The first is regenerated and hand edits are lost. External tool output may
retain that tool's standard `Code generated … DO NOT EDIT.` header so a normal
regeneration stays byte-identical. The second is yours from
here on — edit it freely; Nise will not overwrite it. Ownership is also
readable from the path: `store/`, `openapigen/`, `embedded/`, and any file
ending `.gen.go` are regenerated output.

`nise.json` records the profile, the selected compile-time modules, and the
versions this project was generated with. Deleting it detaches the project
from Nise; nothing else stops working.
